{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And He rested on the seventh day.</b> Here the verse shows wisdom in prohibiting labors which the Sages enumerated as forty labors [less one], among which is a labor that involves no exertion whatsoever in its performance, such as you would say: carrying an object from private domain to public domain or the reverse, and many similar to this — yet the Torah mandated the penalty of death for them. And a person may say: what exertion is there in this thing to warrant liability? Therefore the verse says <b>for six days</b> etc. <b>and He rested</b> etc. — meaning: examine closely and apply reasoning to the rationale, and you will know that the matter is not about exertion alone. For behold, concerning Him of whom it is said (Isaiah 40:28) ״לֹא יִיעַף וְלֹא יִיגָע״, it would not be fitting to speak of rest; rather, the intention is in relation to the action that goes forth we judge — even if it involves no toil or exertion. And therefore anything upon which the designation מלאכה applies, even if a person does it without feeling anything at all, is liable for it." "<b>Further,</b> it intends along this line: <b>for six days did YHWH make</b> etc. — meaning that He did not make the arrangement of their creation to endure except for six days, and from then on YHWH needs to renew the creation every single day. And through the vital force of the soul of the world — which is the Shabbat that YHWH created — He rested from the continuity of labor. And as the Sages of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 10:9), that the world was unstable and trembling, and when Shabbat came it stood firm in its existence. And this is what the verse says: <b>and He rested on the seventh day</b> — and this day sustains the world for another six days. And every Friday evening, when the allotted duration of the world is spent, Shabbat comes and reestablishes the world for another six days, and so on along this pattern. See the portion of Bereshit (Genesis 2:2)."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:31:09.012958994Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}